            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political Views</strong><br>\nCamus was known for his strong stance on moral issues and was a vocal opponent of the death penalty. He was an advocate for social justice and spoke out on political matters, often causing controversy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tragic Death</strong><br>\nCamus died in a car accident on January 4, 1960, at the age of 46. His premature death left his final novel, \"The First Man,\" unfinished, which was published posthumously in 1994.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nCamus's work continues to influence philosophical thought, literature, and discussions on existentialism and absurdism. His writings remain relevant to current debates on ethics, freedom, and the human condition.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
